 First, notice $a$, $b$, $c$ are not zero, so we can divide by $a^2 - bc$, $b^2 - ac$, and $c^2 - ab$ respectively and the denominator is never zero. Next, notice
\begin{align*}
\frac{a^2 b^2}{(a^2-bc)(b^2-ac)} &= \frac{a^2 b^2}{a^2 b^2 - a^3 c - b^3 a + abc^2} \\
&= \frac{a^2 b^2}{a^2 b^2 + abc(a + b - c)} \\
&= \frac{1}{1 + \frac{a + b - c}{ab}}.
\end{align*}
Using $a + b + c = 0,$ we have $c = -a - b.$ Thus,
\begin{align*}
\frac{a^2 b^2}{(a^2-bc)(b^2-ac)} &= \frac{1}{1 - \frac{2}{ab}} \\
\frac{a^2 c^2}{(a^2 - bc)(c^2 - ab)} &= \frac{1}{1 - \frac{2}{ac}} \\
\frac{b^2 c^2}{(b^2 - ac)(c^2 - ab)} &= \frac{1}{1 - \frac{2}{bc}}.
\end{align*}
Then the expression above equals
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{1 - \frac{2}{ab}} + \frac{1}{1 - \frac{2}{ac}} + \frac{1}{1 - \frac{2}{bc}} &= \frac{abc(ab + ac + bc)}{(ab - 2c)(ac - 2b)(bc - 2a)} \\
&= \frac{abc(ab + ac + bc)}{(ab - 2c)(bc + 2a)(ac + 2b)} \\
&= \boxed{1}. \\
\end{align*}
